09-02-2026 OOP in Java

Classes and Objects

OOP is a programming paradigm that organizes software design around data, or objects, rather than functions and logic alone.

Class - blueprint
Object - instance of class
    - Every object has a state (data), behaviour (methods), and Identity (unique)

Convention first letter capital for Classes


Constructor
    - No return type
    - same name as class
    - Intialize starting values / objects in heap
    - Possible to overload constructors (paramaterized vs default)

    Person = new Person("Name", age);

Encapsulation
    - Only methods within a class can access private data
    - Can't access data directly if designated private
    - private data
    - public methods
    - state - data
    - method - behavior

Polymorphism
    - Method overloading is a type of Polymorphism


Getter and Setters
    - Get, returns value, getMethodName
    - Set, sets value, should put in checks for logic (eg setting age, age can't be 0), setMethodName

Inheritance
    - Super / parent classes
    - Sub / child classes
    - super KEYWORD, access superclass members and calls the superclass constructor

Abstract Classes
    - Cannot make objects of abstract classes

Interface
    - Blueprint of a class
    - Class implements interface (rather than extends)

SOLID PRINCIPLES
    - Single Responsibility
    - Open/Closed Principle
    - Liskov Substitution Principle
    - Interface Segregation Principle
    - Dependency Inversion Principle